WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:07.399
In June 1947, Architectural Forum chose a
unique cover star.

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:11.990
The Lustron was a steel frame home with 
steel panels and a steel roof.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:18.720
And a lot of them are still around today.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.170
It was a new idea of home for 
post-World War II America.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.859
It was the future of housing, 
homes made in a factory

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:29.890
and sent around the country 
with an instruction manual

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.710
for every single piece.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.180
Steel on the outside.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.100
Steel on the inside.

00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:41.150
And they made house after house 
after house

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.621
until 1950, when, just 3 years
after that cover shot

00:00:45.621 --> 00:00:48.685
the future of housing became a footnote.

00:00:50.332 --> 00:00:54.472
But the failure of this house isn’t just
an oddity in homebuilding.

00:00:54.472 --> 00:00:58.510
It’s a story of post-war optimism, 
the limits of technology

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.530
and a testing ground for how the government and private industry

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.188
could or couldn't work together.

00:01:17.592 --> 00:01:22.310
In 1940, a typical house would have had a
wood frame and wood and plaster walls.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.329
A lot do today.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:27.946
When you change that to a steel frame and
steel panels things get weird.

00:01:56.926 --> 00:01:58.075
Cool!

00:02:02.224 --> 00:02:03.425
What?

00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:09.825
Most panels in a Lustron home are porcelain
enamel-covered steel.

00:02:09.825 --> 00:02:16.417
When we started treating the house more like
a car than a house, things went better.

00:02:19.144 --> 00:02:23.059
The finish on that is different than 
the finish on this

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:25.977
and the same is true of some other 
parts of the house.

00:02:25.977 --> 00:02:30.780
This is the durable kind of thing 
that's held the test of time.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.590
And then that is...

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.581
I think those tend to get scruffy.

00:02:34.581 --> 00:02:35.919
Imagine a steel panel.

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:39.750
In the factory they melted glass and 
fused it to the steel

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:44.569
creating porcelain enamel, a surprisingly durable, 
creamy surface

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:48.094
that showed up in Lustron homes 
and could be tinted colors:

00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:51.915
maize yellow, surf blue, desert tan, 
and dove gray.

00:02:51.915 --> 00:02:57.607
Though it was meant to look like Spanish tile
the roof was porcelain enameled steel too.

00:02:57.607 --> 00:03:03.280
It covered a ceiling-mounted 
radiant heating system.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.319
Lustron printed instructions for, and made,
all the plumbing too.

00:03:07.714 --> 00:03:09.222
Oh cool...

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:10.438
oh look!

00:03:10.438 --> 00:03:11.608
What, what, what?

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:15.302
Look, it says Lustron corporation on there.

00:03:15.302 --> 00:03:16.932
Oh, oh!

00:03:16.932 --> 00:03:18.655
That's awesome.

00:03:18.655 --> 00:03:22.287
I should get my cell phone and have you take
a picture for me because I can't—

00:03:22.287 --> 00:03:23.752
I'm not tall enough.

00:03:23.752 --> 00:03:27.422
Lustron Corporation, Columbus, Ohio.

00:03:27.422 --> 00:03:29.693
Local Union number 189.

00:03:29.693 --> 00:03:31.787
Oh my gosh.

00:03:31.787 --> 00:03:34.296
Built ins were also a required feature.

00:03:34.296 --> 00:03:37.940
Like this passthrough between the kitchen
and dining room.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.510
So the house is about a thousand square feet
which is small, by modern standards.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:48.280
But in all the houses that were built in the
emerging suburbs in the 50s and 60s were generally

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.930
about this size and one bathroom typically.

00:03:54.717 --> 00:03:57.379
This house is the Westchester 2 bedroom plan

00:03:57.379 --> 00:03:59.891
But there were other configurations as well.

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:04.393
When Life Magazine published a pre-production
Lustron ad in 1948

00:04:04.393 --> 00:04:07.849
thousands of people clipped the coupon in
the lower right corner

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:09.059
to get a free booklet.

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:12.900
Why was this the house America 
has been waiting for?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.050
And what went wrong?

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:22.553
In 1947, Lustron was perfectly situated between
two larger trends.

00:04:22.741 --> 00:04:26.350
After World War II, millions of returning
GIs needed housing

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:32.300
due to the Great Depression and construction
restrictions during the war.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.450
In 1946, Congress declared housing 
a national emergency.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.020
That intersected with funding trends,

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:44.090
private venture capital wouldn’t become
mainstream until the late 1940s and 1950s.

00:04:46.648 --> 00:04:51.470
But a post-Depression agency, the Reconstruction
Finance Corporation

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.722
existed to fund depressed sectors of the economy.

00:04:54.722 --> 00:04:59.860
The “RFC” had already experimented with
funding weird prefabricated home ideas

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.750
Like this concrete building.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.295
An inventor and builder named Carl Strandlund

00:05:04.295 --> 00:05:07.370
who’d previously worked with porcelain enamel
on buildings

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.620
like gas stations and restaurants

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.199
saw his opening in the timeline.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:17.050
By 1947, he’d snagged a $12,500,000 loan
to build out

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.880
the Lustron factory in Columbus Ohio

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.180
a building that today is a
designer shoe warehouse.

00:05:24.726 --> 00:05:26.041
Warehouse.

00:05:26.041 --> 00:05:29.130
Designer shoe warehouse warehouse.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.126
Yeah.

00:05:31.126 --> 00:05:33.810
Life published that hit ad with the coupon

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:39.279
and Popular Mechanics was soon cooing over
“how porcelain skin protects prefab.”

00:05:39.711 --> 00:05:43.289
But by 1948, the house that America 
was talking about

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:45.550
was running into problems.

00:05:46.566 --> 00:05:51.160
The most obvious impediment, the steel, 
wasn’t the only factor.

00:05:51.762 --> 00:05:53.389
Yes, steel is heavy.

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:55.601
A Lustron home needed 12 tons.

00:05:55.601 --> 00:05:59.440
That weight did prevent Lustrons from being
shipped West of the Rockies

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.027
but custom trailers like this one 
helped move the steel.

00:06:03.027 --> 00:06:06.750
Lustron also benefited by getting 
the lion’s share of the

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.903
government allocation of steel 
for prefab homes.

00:06:09.903 --> 00:06:14.323
However, by 1948, Time Magazine was asking
the obvious question:

00:06:14.323 --> 00:06:16.989
Did people want to live in steel houses?

00:06:16.989 --> 00:06:20.350
Especially ones with rigid floorplans 
that were hard to modify.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:25.030
Controlling every element of production also
contributed to price hikes and delays.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:28.316
The Lustron factory made nearly 
every part of the house.

00:06:28.316 --> 00:06:32.800
For one example, they bought a really expensive
press to make bathtubs.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.661
But theirs was 5 feet and a half inch long
instead of the standard 5 feet.

00:06:37.661 --> 00:06:41.080
So they couldn't buy their own tubs 
or sell to others.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.710
That half inch was an expensive mistake 
that never paid off.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:50.560
All this not only increased prices, 
but made those prices non-standard.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.039
By January 1949, Carl Strandlund was in Life
Magazine promoting the home as a revolution

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:59.195
but prices had ballooned up to $10,000.

00:06:59.195 --> 00:07:04.123
That was thousands more than the early ambition
and a lot of money for GIs.

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:06.810
That didn’t stack up well against other
new homes

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.154
that were larger and more traditional.

00:07:09.154 --> 00:07:11.707
Prices also varied from place to place.

00:07:11.707 --> 00:07:16.092
Look at the costs in Indiana, North Carolina,
and Pennsylvania.

00:07:16.092 --> 00:07:18.979
Localization made it tougher in other ways
too.

00:07:18.979 --> 00:07:24.000
Some local codes made it impossible to even
build a steel home, like in Chicago.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.349
Mass developments like this one, in Quantico,
in Virginia,

00:07:27.349 --> 00:07:30.188
were part of the plan, but they ended up 
being rare.

00:07:30.188 --> 00:07:32.766
Even Lustron’s funding became a hindrance.

00:07:32.766 --> 00:07:39.306
As further funding requests ticked up to between
$35 million and $40 million

00:07:39.306 --> 00:07:42.121
Lustron attracted press attention quickly

00:07:42.121 --> 00:07:47.306
for failing to hit goals and 
requiring Government money.

00:07:47.306 --> 00:07:53.889
The 1947 cover model was suddenly in question
by May of 1949.

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:57.322
Scandals that later came to light, like a
paid-for advertorial

00:07:57.322 --> 00:08:00.408
by Senator Joseph McCarthy, 
didn’t help.

00:08:00.408 --> 00:08:03.699
But just having the public eye on the company’s
performance

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:05.842
was probably the greatest challenge.

00:08:05.842 --> 00:08:11.022
By 1950, Lustron’s funding was recalled
and the company entered bankruptcy.

00:08:11.681 --> 00:08:15.955
Over just 2 years, they never sold more than
300 homes a month.

00:08:31.832 --> 00:08:37.860
2.1 miles, or about 3.37 kilometers from the
Designer Shoe Warehouse Warehouse

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:43.360
is the Whitehall Historical Society's headquarters
which was moved there in 2003

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.249
on the site of a former volleyball court.

00:08:50.613 --> 00:08:54.740
Half of it is set up as a home 
and the other half

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.280
is set up as meeting and display space.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.470
This garage contains our restroom and a kitchen.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:03.970
And then the front half of it is workshop
and storage.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:06.260
We don't have a lot of resources.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:09.743
So when it came time for us to try to find
a headquarters

00:09:09.743 --> 00:09:14.670
I knew that the house was for sale on eBay.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.980
I was convinced that we could do this
but I just decided that

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:21.670
it was something we could handle 
by taking it apart

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.635
and putting it back together again.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:30.050
So I approached the owner of the house
and asked if they would donate it to us

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:30.900
which they did.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.531
This house, it was in London, Ohio.

00:09:33.531 --> 00:09:38.870
These are pictures of us taking the house
apart over in London.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:43.130
as we progressed here, it was just getting
to be a big open space inside.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:47.810
We had a dedication day where we had 
a groundbreaking ceremony

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:51.005
and we had a special cake with the 
Lustron emblem on it.

00:09:51.005 --> 00:09:52.005
This is the manual.

00:09:52.005 --> 00:09:53.680
Let me bring this over here.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.361
And we used it for both assembling and 
disassembling the house.

00:09:58.361 --> 00:10:01.880
Lustron had delivered 2,600 homes around the
country

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.570
but fell far short of their ambitions.

00:10:05.511 --> 00:10:07.231
They had failed.

00:10:08.269 --> 00:10:09.307
But they aren't forgotten.

00:10:19.669 --> 00:10:22.225
Thanks for visiting with me in this Lustron
home.

00:10:22.225 --> 00:10:26.130
There were a couple of books that were indispensable
to the research for this project.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:30.912
So, if you want to nerd out more about these
homes, check those out.

00:10:30.912 --> 00:10:33.101
I'll put links to them in the description.

00:10:33.101 --> 00:10:37.986
Also, I really want to thank the commenter
who came up with the idea for this video.

00:10:37.986 --> 00:10:41.747
Yea, it was just a comment saying that Lustron
homes were interesting 

00:10:41.747 --> 00:10:44.290
and everybody at Vox agreed.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:47.060
So yea, thanks for that and if there are any
other pieces

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.480
of interesting or forgotten architecture

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.110
that you think we should cover 
in a future video

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.650
let us know!

